# DECLARED auto-accept exceptions for ROLLING `from-url` rows.
#
# Format: <dest>  accept=auto  verify=<colon-argv|deferred>  declared=<YYYY-MM-DD>
#         expires=<YYYY-MM-DD>  exitcheck=<colon-argv>  tradeoff=<repo-relative doc>
#
# ── WHAT A ROW HERE DOES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# It lets ONE `rolling=` row in `tools/setup/manifests/from-url` install bytes
# whose sha256 disagrees with the pin, instead of aborting `install.sh`. That is
# it. It does not move the pin, does not touch any pin surface, does not write a
# re-measure receipt, and does not make any verifier claim go green.
#
# ── WHAT IT EXPLICITLY DOES NOT DO ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THERE IS NO GLOBAL SWITCH HERE AND THERE MUST NEVER BE ONE. A `*` row matches
# the literal dest `*` and nothing else — `activeExceptionFor` compares dests
# with `===`. Every rolling row without a row below still fails closed, every
# non-rolling row is refused an exception outright by
# `src/Core.TypeScript/hygiene/lint-rolling-exceptions.ts`, and an EXPIRED row
# is inert at install time AND turns the gate red.
#
# ── THE MAINTAINER'S RULING, 2026-09-09 ──────────────────────────────────────
#
#   "okay i think we can just allow for the roll and update for now, i know that
#    is not great from a security point of view but this is not a permanent fix,
#    it just until we don't need the new features anymore, we are going to need
#    this exception path we can just save the exceptions somewhere and let it not
#    break install.sh."
#
# The security cost is real, was accepted knowingly, and is written down in each
# row's `tradeoff=` doc rather than softened. The lint refuses a row whose
# tradeoff doc does not say what an ATTACKER gains while the row is live.
#
# ── THE FIELDS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   accept     `auto` and nothing else. A closed set of one, so a typo is a
#              refusal rather than a fall-through to permissive.
#   verify     colon-encoded argv run under `bun` BEFORE the bytes are accepted.
#              Non-zero ⇒ the bytes are REFUSED and the failure is recorded.
#              `deferred` is the honest way to say nothing runs — and it is
#              recorded as `verdict=not-run`, never as a pass.
#   declared   the day the row was written. Bounds how far `expires` may reach.
#   expires    the day it stops working. At most 90 days after `declared`;
#              past ⇒ inert at install time and red in the gate.
#   exitcheck  colon-encoded argv falsifying the row's stated PREMISE — the
#              reason the exception exists at all. Run in the gate, so "the
#              reason evaporated" is a red check rather than a silence.
#   tradeoff   the doc carrying the security analysis.
#
# ── WHERE ACCEPTANCES ARE RECORDED ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `.zeta/from-url-rolling-accepts` — untracked, per machine, append-only,
# idempotent. Every acceptance names both digests, the clock, and whether a
# verification ran and what it said. It is NOT `from-url-rolling-receipts`:
# accepting is not measuring, and only a receipt written by
# `tools/setup/repin-rolling.ts` after a re-measure exits 0 can move a pin.
#
# 081M24396B2087G0R000MDMDEA
#
# ── src/Core.TLA/tla2tools.jar ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHY THIS ROW EXISTS. tlaplus republishes the v1.8.0 prerelease asset from CI
# on pushes to master. MEASURED 2026-09-09: FOUR distinct builds served in one
# day (12:48 rev 65fbace · 15:25 rev 4ad12e8 · 16:25 rev 4ad12e8, same rev and
# different bytes · 21:30 rev ede5b88). Each one aborts the whole `from-url`
# realizer — which is not best-effort, so `install.sh` stops — on every machine
# whose cached jar is not the pinned one. That is fleet-wide provisioning
# breakage several times a day for one dependency.
#
# WHY NOT JUST PIN v1.7.4 AND BE DONE. Because v1.7.4 prints the generic
# "Temporal properties were violated." where v1.8.0 names WHICH property, and
# `registry/tlc-models.json` pins the named form as an expectDetail. The
# `exitcheck=` below is that premise made falsifiable: if no gate-tier model
# needs a named temporal property any more, it goes red and this row should be
# retired in favour of an immutable pin.
#
# WHY verify=deferred RATHER THAN THE SWEEP. MEASURED 2026-09-09 on an
# Apple-Silicon host with OpenJDK 26: `run-tlc.ts --all` is 52 models in
# **5m53s** wall clock (and all 52 agreed with their pins on the ede5b88 build,
# so this is the cost of a PASSING sweep, not a pathological one). Six minutes
# added to every `install.sh` on every machine for one dependency is not
# affordable, and it could not run here even if it were: the sweep judges the
# jar against `registry/tlc-models.json`'s versionBanner, which is a COMMITTED
# restatement of the pin, and the accept path is not allowed to move committed
# pin surfaces. Deferring it is therefore not laziness but the only honest
# option — and the deferral is not a promise, it is `repin-rolling.ts`, which
# runs exactly that sweep and refuses to write a digest it did not pass.
#
# EXPIRES 90 DAYS OUT, THE CEILING, DELIBERATELY. The exit condition — a STABLE
# tlaplus release carrying the named-property diagnostic — is not ours to
# schedule, so a shorter window would buy a renewal ritual rather than new
# information. The ceiling is what makes the row revisitable at all.
# RETIRED 2026-09-10. This dest now carries `sha256=tag-only` in `from-url`, so there
# is no digest for an exception to auto-accept and this row could never fire again.
# An exception that cannot fire is a check that cannot fail, which is the thing this
# repo refuses hardest -- so it is deleted rather than left as reassuring ceremony.
#
# The exposure did not change. The row already granted "whoever can publish to that tag
# chooses the verifier this repo runs"; the tag-only pin says the same thing in one
# field instead of five, without an expiry that re-blocks the fleet on a date nobody
# chose. See the tagonly= reason on the from-url row.
